---
import StarlightPage from '@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro';
import EngineProbe from '../components/EngineProbe';
---

<StarlightPage
  frontmatter={{
    title: 'Live demos',
    description:
      'Where the engine will run in your browser, and the island seam waiting for it.',
    tableOfContents: false,
  }}
>
  <p>
    fleuron's two claims — that a book paginates in hundreds of milliseconds,
    and that the preview is exactly the export — are ones you should be able to
    check here, on your own machine. Not yet: the WebAssembly bindings and the
    SVG painter have not landed, so there is nothing on this page to run.
  </p>

  <p>
    The panel below is the seam they will mount into: a React island hydrated
    with <code>client:visible</code>, so it ships no JavaScript until it scrolls
    into view, and none at all to any other page.
  </p>

  <div class="not-content probe-frame">
    <EngineProbe client:visible />
  </div>

  <p>
    Prose pages ship no JavaScript, which is why the site is built this way. A
    demo carrying a WebAssembly engine can afford to be expensive as long as
    nothing else on the site is.
  </p>
</StarlightPage>

<style is:global>
  .probe-frame {
    margin: 2rem 0;
    padding: 1.5rem;
    background: var(--f-glass-solid);
    border: 1px solid var(--f-hairline);
    border-radius: var(--f-radius);
  }

  .probe-state {
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
    font-family: var(--f-mono);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--f-text);
  }

  .probe-state span {
    color: var(--f-ember);
  }

  .probe-note {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--f-text-muted);
  }
</style>
